     1	# Silent Collapse: A Distribution-Shift Teardown of a Production Driving Model and a Zero-Retraining Recurrent-State Monitor
     2	
     3	**Yusuf Guenena**
     4	Wayne State University
     5	Code and data: https://github.com/yusufdxb/supercombo-blindspot
     6	
     7	---
     8	
     9	## Abstract
    10	
    11	Production Level-2 driver-assistance stacks are validated largely in simulation, and that
    12	practice rests on an unstated assumption: that the shipped model behaves the same on rendered
    13	input as on real input, or at least signals when it does not. We test that assumption on one
    14	deployed model, openpilot v0.9.7 supercombo, the network that drives comma hardware on public
    15	roads. We first build a parity-exact reimplementation of its inference path, verified to within
    16	+/-0.5 m/s^2 of comma's own reference output on 100% of 1159 real-footage frames (median
    17	absolute delta 0.0409 m/s^2), so that any downstream anomaly is attributable to the model and
    18	not to our harness. Running the verified model on CARLA-rendered clean roads, we find that it
    19	fails silently: 8 of 10 output heads collapse to under 1% of their real-driving temporal
    20	activity and the 512-D recurrent feature freezes to about 1e-5 of its real spread, while the
    21	model's own predictive-uncertainty heads rise only 1.20x to 1.84x and not one out-of-distribution
    22	frame (0 of 219) crosses the real-driving 95th percentile. Nothing the model emits flags the
    23	collapse. An alpha-blend sweep characterizes the collapse as a hard cliff on the Subaru source
    24	(transition width 0.015), though the cliff shape is segment-dependent (a gradient of width 0.274
    25	on the RAM source), and a layer-by-layer probe localizes it downstream of the vision encoder, in
    26	the recurrent summarizer and action-block feedback path, not in perception. We then show that the
    27	signal the outputs hide is recoverable from the model's own recurrent feature with a single
    28	second-order statistic, the rolling temporal spread of the 512-D state: one O(d) quantity per
    29	forward pass, with no retraining and no architecture change. Calibrated leave-one-corpus-out to
    30	about a 1% real-driving false-positive rate (N=2, a two-fold estimate, not a production FPR), it
    31	separates the collapse at AUROC 0.996 [0.992, 1.000] and fires about 0.23 blend-units before the
    32	outputs cliff, where the location-based feature scores one would default to (Mahalanobis,
    33	Relative Mahalanobis, KNN) each hit 100% leave-one-corpus-out FPR and fail to transfer across the
    34	two real corpora. This is a single-model negative finding with a collapse-specific monitor, not a
    35	general OOD detector: an ImageNet-C sweep shows the silent collapse is sim-specific (no corruption
    36	reproduces it; at most 1 of 10 heads collapses on any of 75 corruption-severity cells, versus 7
    37	of 10 under CARLA), and the monitor is near chance on most photometric corruptions. The
    38	contribution is that output-side and location-based signals alone are insufficient for this one
    39	shipped model's safety case, and that a second-order recurrent-state monitor is a cheap complement
    40	that the present evidence does not claim generalizes.
    41	
    42	---
    43	
    44	## 1. Introduction
    45	
    46	Most Level-2 and autonomous-driving programs validate their driving policy largely in simulation,
    47	because simulation is a primary setting in which rare and dangerous scenarios can be exercised at
    48	scale and at low cost. The validity of that practice rests on an assumption that is rarely tested
    49	directly in the literature we surveyed: that the shipped model under test behaves on rendered input the
    50	way it behaves on real input, or, failing that, that it fails loudly enough for the test harness
    51	or a downstream safety monitor to notice. If a model can be shown a simulated scene and quietly
    52	stop doing its job while every output it emits still looks plausible, then a passing simulation is
    53	not evidence that the model works. It is evidence that the model produced a safe-looking default,
    54	and the two are indistinguishable from the outside.
    55	
    56	We test that assumption on a single deployed model, openpilot v0.9.7 supercombo, the end-to-end
    57	network in comma's shipped openpilot driver-assistance system (Chen et al. 2022), and we find the dangerous answer. On
    58	CARLA-rendered clean roads, 8 of 10 of the model's output heads collapse to under 1% of their
    59	real-driving temporal activity, and the 512-D recurrent state that threads the model's memory
    60	across frames freezes to roughly 1e-5 of its real spread. Yet the model's own predictive-uncertainty
    61	heads rise only 1.20x to 1.84x, and not one out-of-distribution frame, 0 of 219, exceeds the
    62	95th-percentile uncertainty the model exhibits in real driving. The failure is silent by the
    63	model's own signals: the planning trajectory, the acceleration command, and the lane and lead
    64	geometry all go dark, but the channel a safety case would watch to catch exactly that event stays
    65	quiet.
    66	
    67	The failure mode this exposes is not hypothetical. Phantom braking under distribution shift, the
    68	model commanding a deceleration for an obstacle that is not there, is a known and user-reported
    69	failure of the shipped openpilot stack, documented in the project's own issue tracker (commaai
    70	issue #20704). We cite that report as motivation only; we make no causal claim linking the silent
    71	collapse we measure in simulation to any specific field braking event. The point it grounds is
    72	narrower and sufficient: a simulation "pass" can be the model having collapsed to a safe-looking
    73	default rather than the model perceiving the scene, and the output-side and uncertainty signals a
    74	downstream safety case would trust are precisely the signals that stay silent when it does. That
    75	is the gap.
    76	
    77	The closest published monitors do not close it. The nearest AV-native neighbor watches the feature
    78	density of a frozen perception encoder one stage upstream of where the collapse lives (Keser et al.
    79	2025), and the next-nearest watches the latent dynamics of a standalone trajectory predictor with
    80	provable changepoint guarantees (Guo and Su 2026); neither targets the recurrent state of a shipped
    81	end-to-end driver. A recent line in language and vision-language models, EigenTrack (arXiv:2509.15735),
    82	does stream a second-order statistic of hidden activations through a trained classifier with early
    83	warning, but on LLMs and VLMs, not on a driving model, and not under cross-corpus transfer. And the
    84	standard location-based feature-space scores one would reach for first (Mahalanobis, Relative
    85	Mahalanobis, KNN) each hit 100% leave-one-corpus-out false-positive rate on this model: calibrated
    86	on one of our two real corpora, they flag the entirety of the other.
    87	
    88	This paper makes four contributions, each bounded to the single model under study. First, a
    89	parity-exact reimplementation of openpilot v0.9.7 supercombo inference, verified to within
    90	+/-0.5 m/s^2 of comma's reference output on 100% of 1159 real frames (median absolute delta
    91	0.0409 m/s^2), including two non-obvious correctness details (recurrent-state threading and
    92	unnormalized YUV input) that a faithful reimplementation must get right. Second, an empirical
    93	demonstration of silent failure under visual distribution shift on this model: simultaneous
    94	output-head collapse, recurrent-feature freeze, and a non-responsive uncertainty channel, with
    95	0 of 219 OOD frames exceeding the model's real-driving uncertainty p95. Third, a characterization
    96	of the collapse as a hard cliff on the Subaru source (transition width 0.015) whose shape is
    97	segment-dependent (a gradient of width 0.274 on the RAM source), localized downstream of the
    98	vision encoder in the recurrent summarizer and action-block feedback path (a partial localization;
    99	an ambiguity in the summarizer's variational bottleneck remains). Fourth, a zero-retraining monitor
   100	on the model's own recurrent feature, the rolling temporal spread of the 512-D state, calibrated
   101	leave-one-corpus-out to about a 1% real-driving FPR (N=2, a two-fold estimate), which separates the
   102	collapse at AUROC 0.996 and fires about 0.23 blend-units before the output cliff, where the
   103	location-based scores fail to transfer; bounded by an ImageNet-C sweep that shows the collapse is
   104	sim-specific and the monitor collapse-specific, not a universal OOD detector. Figure 1 previews all
   105	four findings.
   106	
   107	---
   108	
   109	## 2. Related Work
   110	
   111	This contribution sits at the intersection of two ancestral lines. One is location-based
   112	feature-space OOD detection, the family that this paper runs as baselines and shows fails to
   113	transfer across corpora rather than fails to separate within one. The other is internal-activation
   114	runtime monitoring, the family the proposed monitor belongs to, pushed onto the recurrent state of
   115	a shipped driver. We name the neighbors a reviewer will invoke and state, for each, exactly what it
   116	lacks that this work has. Table RW compresses the comparison onto five axes.
   117	
   118	**Location-based feature-space OOD (the baseline family).** The output-side floor is maximum
   119	softmax probability (Hendrycks and Gimpel 2017) and its energy-based successor (Liu et al. 2020);
   120	the feature-space ancestor is the Mahalanobis distance-from-fitted-Gaussian-mean score (Lee et al.
   121	2018), refined for near-OOD by the relative Mahalanobis distance (Ren et al. 2021), and made
   122	non-parametric by deep nearest-neighbor distance (Sun et al. 2022). ViM (Wang et al. 2022) is the
   123	modern feature-residual-plus-logit hybrid, and Mahalanobis++ (Mueller and Hein 2025) keeps the
   124	feature-Gaussian family live in 2025 with an l2-normalization fix, which is why running the Lee
   125	2018 score here is a fair current comparison and not a strawman. OpenOOD (Yang et al. 2022) codifies
   126	this whole line into one taxonomy and codebase; it is the vocabulary anchor for our baselines, not a
   127	leaderboard this paper ranks on. We run Mahalanobis, relative Mahalanobis, and KNN-50 as baselines
   128	on the same 512-D feature our monitor reads. The honest result, developed in Section 5.6, is that
   129	KNN-50 ties our monitor on single-corpus separation (AUROC 1.000); the distinguishing axis is
   130	cross-corpus calibration, where all three location-based scores hit 100% leave-one-corpus-out FPR.
   131	MSP and Energy are output-side scores on a model whose output channel Section 5.3 shows is silent,
   132	and ViM requires a classifier weight matrix that supercombo's multi-head regression outputs do not
   133	provide; these three are structurally inapplicable, and we name and excuse them rather than omit
   134	them.
   135	
   136	**AV-native OOD and uncertainty monitoring.** The closest published neighbor, Keser et al. 2025,
   137	monitors the feature density of a frozen vision foundation-model encoder as an in-distribution
   138	score; that is one stage upstream of our substrate and is exactly the location-based class our
   139	baselines instantiate. The next-closest, Guo and Su 2026, monitors the latent dynamics of a
   140	standalone trajectory predictor as a quickest-changepoint-detection problem with provable bounds on
   141	detection delay and false alarm; it differs from this work in model class (a standalone predictor,
   142	not a shipped end-to-end driver) and in evidence basis (a theoretical guarantee, not an empirical
   143	calibration). Filos et al. 2020 is the canonical framing of distribution shift for autonomous
   144	vehicles, and the SelfOracle line (Stocco et al. 2020) and its uncertainty-quantification successor
   145	(Grewal, Tonella, and Stocco 2024) build misbehaviour predictors on the assumption that the model's
   146	confidence signal is informative; Section 5.3 is the contrary finding for this model. A 2025 position
   147	paper frames OOD detection explicitly as safety-case evidence (Hodge et al. 2025), which is the niche
   148	this work speaks to. None of these targets the recurrent state of a shipped end-to-end driver under
   149	cross-corpus transfer.
   150	
   151	**Internal-activation and second-order monitors (the proposed monitor's family).** Runtime neuron
   152	activation pattern monitoring (Cheng et al. 2018) is the ancestor of internal-state monitoring: it
   153	stores binarized neuron patterns and compares them by Hamming distance, on feed-forward classifiers,
   154	a first-order discrete per-frame check. An early move to a higher-order feature statistic for OOD
   155	is the Gram-matrix method (Sastry and Oore 2020), the closest lineage point to a second-order rather
   156	than distance-from-mean choice. The proposed monitor inherits the higher-order-statistic idea from
   157	the latter and the internal-monitor idea from the former, but applies them to a recurrent state
   158	(neither did) on a shipped production driving model (neither did), and it is the statistic that
   159	catches the freeze mode, the recurrent spread crashing across the cliff, that a per-frame pattern or
   160	Gram check on outputs would miss. The single closest paper on mechanism is EigenTrack
   161	(arXiv:2509.15735), a parallel and near-simultaneous line that streams covariance-spectrum statistics
   162	(eigenvalue gaps, spectral entropy, random-matrix-theory features) of hidden activations through a
   163	trained recurrent classifier, with early warning, to flag hallucination and OOD drift. It rhymes with
   164	this work on three counts (second-order, hidden-state, fires before surface errors), and it differs
   165	on three checkable ones: substrate (LLMs and VLMs, not a driving model), statistic (a full
   166	eigenspectrum fed to a trained classifier, not a single location-invariant trace with a calibrated
   167	threshold), and evaluation (language OOD benchmarks, not cross-corpus leave-one-corpus-out FPR on a
   168	recurrent driver state). We therefore do not claim to be first to use a second-order hidden-activation
   169	statistic for OOD detection: EigenTrack pre-dates this work on that framing. The available and
   170	defensible claim is narrower: this is the first second-order recurrent-state monitor on a shipped
   171	end-to-end driving model evaluated under cross-corpus leave-one-corpus-out transfer. NECO (Ben Ammar
   172	et al. 2024) builds on a neural-collapse property of classification heads, which supercombo's
   173	regression heads lack; we name and excuse it rather than run it.
   174	
   175	**openpilot and supercombo prior work.** The reference academic teardown of supercombo is
   176	Openpilot-Deepdive (Chen et al. 2022), a static input, output, and architecture analysis plus a
   177	reimplementation; this paper extends that static teardown to a runtime distribution-shift teardown.
   178	The directed-falsification line on openpilot is von Stein and Elbaum (ASE 2022), which generates
   179	adversarial inputs that violate stated properties; it is related testing work and motivation, not a
   180	silent-collapse or recurrent-state-monitor study. A recent adversarial study (arXiv:2505.11532)
   181	targets supercombo with deliberate perturbations and input-level defenses, a different failure mode
   182	(adversarial attack, not sim-rendered silent collapse) with no recurrent-state monitor.
   183	
   184	**Simulation testing of driving DNNs and corruption robustness.** The DeepRoad line uses
   185	metamorphic and generative test synthesis: DeepTest (Tian et al. 2018) produces transformed driving
   186	images to surface erroneous behaviors, DeepRoad (Zhang et al. 2018) uses GANs to render the same
   187	scene under new weather and checks for consistent behavior, and MarMot (Ayerdi et al. 2024) applies
   188	metamorphic relations at runtime. These methods generate or transform driving scenes and test the
   189	model for consistent behavior, implicitly treating the generated input as a valid scene the model
   190	should handle; this paper inverts that premise by showing the simulated input can itself be out of
   191	distribution to the model, which undercuts coverage claims built on sim-based testing.
   192	ImageNet-C (Hendrycks and Dietterich 2019) is the corruption-robustness yardstick we use as the
   193	bounding OOD axis in Section 5.7, with Cityscapes-C (Michaelis et al. 2019) as its AV extension, and
   194	CARLA (Dosovitskiy et al. 2017) is the simulator that supplies the primary OOD axis.
   195	
   196	---
   197	
   198	## 3. Threat Model
   199	
   200	A safety case for a shipped driving model leans on a small set of runtime defenses, and the finding
   201	of this paper is that the specific failure mode it documents defeats exactly those defenses, silently
   202	and simultaneously. We define the threat precisely, walk through why each standard defense misses it,
   203	and position the proposed monitor as a cheap complementary layer rather than a replacement for any of
   204	them.
   205	
   206	**The threat.** A shipped driving model is deployed in a visually shifted context: a rendered
   207	simulator, an unfamiliar geography, a degraded or unusual sensor condition. Under sufficient shift,
   208	three things happen at once and without warning. The output heads collapse to a plausible, nearly
   209	constant signal; the recurrent state that carries the model's temporal memory freezes; and the
   210	model's own predictive-uncertainty channel does not rise. The danger is not that the model is wrong,
   211	which a safety case expects and plans for, but that it is wrong while every signal a monitor would
   212	read says it is fine.
   213	
   214	**Why uncertainty-head monitoring misses it.** The most direct defense is to threshold the model's
   215	own predictive uncertainty: if the model says it is unsure, intervene. On this model that defense
   216	never fires. Under the collapse, predictive uncertainty rises only 1.20x to 1.84x across the
   217	monitored heads, and 0 of 219 OOD frames exceeds the 95th-percentile uncertainty the model exhibits
   218	in real driving (Section 5.3). A threshold calibrated on real driving, which is the only honest way
   219	to set it, never trips. This directly contradicts the SelfOracle and uncertainty-quantification line,
   220	which builds misbehaviour predictors on the premise that the confidence signal carries the

 succeeded in 0ms:
   220	which builds misbehaviour predictors on the premise that the confidence signal carries the
   221	information.
   222	
   223	**Why output-plausibility and temporal-jitter monitors miss it.** A second defense checks that the
   224	model's outputs are physically plausible: bounded acceleration, feasible curvature, a sane plan. The
   225	collapsed outputs are plausible by construction. The plan head retains 0.6% of its real activity and
   226	the acceleration head 0.4%, so the model emits a smooth, near-constant trajectory that reads as a
   227	benign, stationary scene, and a plausibility check passes it. A third defense watches for temporal
   228	jitter or output disagreement as a sign of instability. The freeze produces the opposite of jitter:
   229	a frozen output has lower temporal variance than an active one, so a jitter monitor reads the collapse
   230	as increased stability, the very thing it is built to reward. (These are structural arguments from the
   231	measured activity ratios, not separate experiments.)
   232	
   233	**Why same-architecture ensembles and input-quality checks miss it.** A fourth defense runs an
   234	ensemble and flags disagreement. Section 5.5 localizes the collapse downstream of the vision encoder,
   235	in a path every instance of the same architecture shares, so an ensemble of the same model would
   236	collapse together rather than disagree. A fifth defense screens the input itself for quality. The
   237	CARLA-clean renders are typically sharper and less noisy than real road footage, so an image-quality
   238	screen would rate the simulated input as good, not anomalous. (Structural arguments, not new experiments.)
   239	
   240	**The complementary signal.** The defenses above all read the output side or the input side. The
   241	finding that makes a complement possible is that the model's own recurrent features carry the OOD
   242	signal its outputs do not surface. The proposed monitor (Section 5.6) is a complement, not a
   243	replacement: it is one O(d) statistic computed from a forward pass that already runs, it requires no
   244	retraining and no architecture change, and it is calibrated against a real-driving false-positive rate
   245	rather than against simulated negatives. It is also bounded, and we state the bounds here so they
   246	travel with the proposal: it is collapse-specific (Section 5.7), it is demonstrated offline only, and
   247	its false-positive rate is an N=2 two-fold estimate, not a production number.
   248	
   249	---
   250	
   251	## 4. Method
   252	
   253	This section describes the parity-verified harness, the data, the metrics, the monitor design, and
   254	the baseline set in enough detail to trust and reproduce the teardown. The load-bearing content is
   255	the parity number and the design descriptions; all figures live in Section 5.
   256	
   257	**Parity-exact reimplementation.** We reconstruct openpilot v0.9.7 supercombo inference from the
   258	released ONNX model and comma's own reference files (modeld, the output parser, the YUV loader kernel,
   259	and the model constants). Because the central result is a negative finding about a production model,
   260	the harness must be trustworthy before any anomaly can be attributed to the model rather than to the
   261	reimplementation, so we establish parity first.
   262	
   263	**Recurrent-state threading.** supercombo is a temporal model: it consumes a buffer of recent
   264	features and its own previous desired-curvature output, and it must roll that state forward by
   265	shift-and-append after each inference, with a zero initialization only on the first frame. A naive
   266	per-frame zero reset of the state produces a multi-second initialization transient that, in this
   267	model, looks exactly like a spurious deceleration, a self-inflicted phantom brake of the
   268	reimplementation rather than the model. Getting this right is a precondition for both parity and the
   269	collapse measurement, because every collapse and corruption sequence is re-run with the same correct
   270	state handling.
   271	
   272	**Unnormalized YUV input.** The model's input loader (loadyuv) converts the camera Y, U, and V
   273	channels to float with no rescaling: the model consumes uint8 values in the range 0 to 255, not
   274	values divided by 255. Dividing by 255, the reflexive normalization, shifts the entire input
   275	distribution and silently degrades parity. We feed unnormalized uint8 YUV, matching the kernel.
   276	
   277	**Parity result.** On 1159 real-footage frames (after a 40-frame, 2-second warm-up trim), our
   278	reimplemented longitudinal-acceleration output matches comma's logged reference within +/-0.5 m/s^2
   279	on 100.00% of frames, with a median absolute delta of 0.0409 m/s^2, a mean of 0.0541, and a worst-case
   280	single-frame delta of 0.2899 m/s^2 (no frame exceeds 0.5). This is the load-bearing harness-trust
   281	claim for the negative result, and we report it prominently.
   282	
   283	**Data.** The real in-distribution data are two comma corpora, denoted subaru and ram, of 320 frames
   284	each, with the first 100 frames discarded as warm-up. After warm-up and the rolling-window step, 219
   285	analysis frames remain per corpus. The out-of-distribution data are CARLA-rendered clean-road frames
   286	from the openpilot v0.9.7 simulation pipeline. The sim camera is configured as a matched-intrinsics
   287	pinhole: it renders at the comma 3 fcam native resolution (1928x1208) at the field of view that
   288	reproduces fcam's focal length (2648 px), so the rendered frames carry exactly the production camera
   289	(`_ar_ox_config.fcam`) intrinsics, and with the camera mounted on the device axes the warp to the model
   290	frame reduces to the same intrinsic remap applied to real footage (`src/sim_preprocessor.py`). The
   291	distribution shift is therefore confined to rendered image content (photometry, texture, and the
   292	absence of sensor noise), while camera geometry, calibration, and model-input preprocessing are held
   293	identical to the parity-verified real path; the collapse is a response to rendered scene content, not
   294	an artifact of a mismatched camera model. The interpolation axis for the cliff analysis
   295	(Section 5.4) is a pixel-space alpha-blend of a real sequence (Subaru or RAM) with the CARLA sequence,
   296	with alpha=0 the real frame and alpha=1 the CARLA frame, swept over 29 alpha values. We state the
   297	underlying counts wherever a percentage appears: for example, the "0%" of Section 5.3 is 0 of 219
   298	CARLA frames. For the threshold-free metrics (Section 5.6), the in-distribution set is subaru and ram
   299	concatenated (n=638 stored frames) and the out-of-distribution set is the alpha=1.0 CARLA frames
   300	(n=319 stored frames); the rolling-window warm-up leaves 609 and 290 valid (non-NaN) scores
   301	respectively, and the threshold-free metrics are computed on those valid subsets.
   302	
   303	**Metrics.** Output activity is the sum of per-element temporal standard deviation over a window; a
   304	head is "collapsed" when its CARLA-to-real activity ratio is small. Feature spread is the trace of the
   305	recurrent-state covariance over a rolling window. Detection is scored threshold-free with AUROC, AUPR,
   306	and FPR at 95% TPR, each with a stratified bootstrap 95% confidence interval (n=1000 iterations,
   307	seed 42). Calibration uses a leave-one-corpus-out (LOCO) protocol across the two real corpora: a
   308	threshold is set on one corpus and its false-positive rate is read on the held-out corpus, then the
   309	two folds are averaged. Because there are only two real corpora, every LOCO FPR is a two-fold estimate
   310	whose variance is not meaningfully reportable, and we state it as such every time it appears.
   311	
   312	**The monitor (E6).** The monitored quantity is the rolling temporal spread of the 512-D recurrent
   313	feature the model emits, computed as the trace of the covariance of a 30-frame window of the state.
   314	The detection threshold is the 1st percentile of the real-driving rolling-spread distribution, which
   315	targets a roughly 1% false-positive rate by construction, and it is calibrated leave-one-corpus-out.
   316	The monitor adds one O(d) statistic per forward pass, with no retraining and no extra heads, and its
   317	statistic is location-invariant (a second-order spread), which is the property that distinguishes it
   318	from the location-based baseline family.
   319	
   320	**Baselines and structural exclusions.** The three applicable post-hoc feature-space scores,
   321	Mahalanobis, relative Mahalanobis (RMD), and KNN-50, are computed on the same 512-D feature the
   322	monitor reads, with a PCA-Mahalanobis variant as an ablation. RMD's background distribution is fit as
   323	a two-component Gaussian mixture, because with a single in-distribution class the Ren et al. marginal
   324	Gaussian degenerates to the class Gaussian and the relative score collapses to zero. MSP, Energy, and
   325	ViM are structurally inapplicable to supercombo's multi-head Gaussian-mixture regression and
   326	existence-probability outputs (there is no softmax head, no logit vector, and no classifier weight
   327	matrix), and we state this explicitly so reviewers read it as an exclusion with a reason, not an
   328	omission.
   329	
   330	---
   331	
   332	## 5. Experiments and Results
   333	
   334	The experiments unfold in the order the argument requires: parity establishes trust (Section 4),
   335	collapse establishes the phenomenon (E1), the freeze and the silent uncertainty channel establish the
   336	gap (E2, E3), the cliff sweep characterizes the shape (E4), the layer probe localizes the mechanism
   337	(E5), the monitor and baselines deliver the solution (E6), and the corruption sweep bounds the scope
   338	(E7).
   339	
   340	### 5.1 E1: Output collapse
   341	
   342	We run the parity-verified model on CARLA-rendered clean roads and measure, for each of the 10 output
   343	heads, the ratio of its CARLA temporal activity to its real-driving temporal activity. Eight of the
   344	ten heads collapse to under 1% of real activity: desired_curv (0.0018), accel_t0 (0.0040), lead
   345	(0.0042), desire_state (0.0049), lane_lines (0.0054), plan (0.0057), lead_prob (0.0058), and
   346	road_edges (0.0076). These eight are the safety-critical driving signals: the planning trajectory, the
   347	acceleration command, the lane and road-edge geometry, the lead-vehicle detection, and the curvature
   348	command. Two heads survive: pose (0.1788) and meta (0.7181), the ego-motion and meta-state outputs.
   349	The model's primary driving outputs are functionally inactive on CARLA-rendered input while its
   350	ego-motion and meta outputs partially persist. Figure E1 plots the per-head ratios.
   351	
   352	### 5.2 E2: Recurrent-feature freeze
   353	
   354	We measure the rolling covariance trace of the 512-D recurrent hidden state on real versus CARLA
   355	frames. On CARLA the feature spread falls to about 1e-5 (0.00001x) of its real value: the recurrent
   356	state freezes to a near-constant vector. Despite that freeze, the frozen vector is linearly separable
   357	from the real-driving states at 87.9% along the centroid-difference direction (d' = 2.19), which
   358	establishes that the recurrent state carries a strong out-of-distribution signal even while the output
   359	heads are dark. This is the signal the monitor of Section 5.6 reads. It also previews the mechanism
   360	that explains the Mahalanobis failure in Section 5.6: the frozen vector lands near the high-density
   361	center of the in-distribution Gaussian, so a distance-from-mean score reads it as in-distribution.
   362	Figure E2 shows the projected feature space with the CARLA freeze point.
   363	
   364	### 5.3 E3: Uncertainty silence
   365	
   366	This is the safety-relevant centerpiece. For three representative heads we report, side by side, the
   367	fraction of output activity retained on CARLA, the ratio of CARLA to real predictive uncertainty, and
   368	the fraction of the 219 CARLA frames that exceed the real-driving 95th-percentile uncertainty for that
   369	head. The plan head retains 0.6% of its activity while its uncertainty rises 1.35x; the lead head
   370	retains 0.4% at 1.20x; the desired_curv head retains 0.2% at 1.84x. In all three cases, 0 of 219 CARLA
   371	frames exceeds the real-driving p95. The outputs lose roughly 99.5% of their activity, but the
   372	uncertainty channel barely moves and never crosses the threshold a real-calibrated monitor would set.
   373	
   374	The implication is the gap this paper turns on. A safety monitor that thresholds the model's own
   375	uncertainty, calibrated on real driving, never fires under the collapse, because nothing the model
   376	emits flags it. We state this strictly as an empirical finding about supercombo v0.9.7 under CARLA
   377	input, that the uncertainty channel stays quiet, and we draw no causal line from it to any field
   378	phantom-braking incident. Figure E3 plots the uncertainty distributions for real and CARLA frames
   379	against the real-driving p95 line, making the silence visually unambiguous.
   380	
   381	### 5.4 E4: Cliff characterization and segment dependence
   382	
   383	To characterize the shape of the collapse, we sweep the pixel-space alpha-blend from the real frame
   384	(alpha=0) toward the CARLA frame (alpha=1). On the Subaru source the response is two-phase. Output
   385	activity first balloons to 6.32x of the real baseline at alpha=0.425, a thrash driven by the
   386	ghosted-input interference of the half-blended frame, and then collapses through a hard cliff, falling
   387	from 0.9x to 0.1x of real activity over the narrow alpha band 0.784 to 0.799, a transition width of
   388	0.015. The feature spread crashes from 0.25 to 0.00 by about alpha=0.78. Through the entire transition
   389	the predictive uncertainty never spikes, consistent with E3.
   390	
   391	The cliff shape is segment-dependent, and this is a bound on the early-warning headroom the monitor
   392	exploits. On the RAM source the same sweep produces a gradient, not a cliff: output activity falls from
   393	0.9x to 0.1x of real over the wide alpha band 0.666 to 0.940, a transition width of 0.274. On the RAM
   394	source the monitor's firing point (alpha=0.850) lands inside that band rather than before it, so the
   395	early-warning headroom is negative (-0.184) where on Subaru it is positive (0.234). Cliff headroom
   396	therefore cannot be assumed to generalize across segments; the characterization is partial. We use the
   397	authoritative `report/e4_results.md` and `report/e4_ram_results.md` values (Subaru width 0.015, RAM
   398	width 0.274) throughout; where a figure legend rounds these (for example a Subaru legend reading
   399	"0.02"), the results-file value governs. Figure E4 places the Subaru cliff and the RAM gradient side by
   400	side.
   401	
   402	### 5.5 E5: Localization downstream of the vision encoder
   403	
   404	We first ask whether the collapse is the vision encoder failing, by measuring the CARLA-to-real
   405	activity ratio of each encoder stage across the alpha sweep. It is not. Every encoder stage stays at or
   406	above real activity across the full sweep: at alpha=1 the stem is at 1.43x, stage3 at 2.06x, and the
   407	head at 2.14x, and the minimum ratio over all stages and all alpha is about 0.96. No encoder stage ever
   408	crosses the 0.5 collapse threshold. The encoder is in fact more active on CARLA than on real input, so
   409	the collapse seen at the output is not perception failing; it originates downstream. The structural
   410	statement is explicit: the failure is in the summarizer and action block, not in the encoder.
   411	
   412	We then probe eight tensors between the summarizer and the per-head outputs. Two cross the 0.5 collapse
   413	threshold. The recurrent summarizer's variational bottleneck (summarizer_div, which is the hidden_state
   414	the monitor reads) has a cliff at alpha=0.900, with its mean shifting to 0.023 of real, a near
   415	two-order-of-magnitude collapse of the rolling mean of the 512-D vector; this is the entry point of the
   416	collapse. The action-block body (action_block_body), the last residual block before the curvature head,
   417	has a cliff a full alpha step earlier, at alpha=0.500, because it folds in the model's own previous
   418	desired-curvature output through the recurrent feedback loop, so once that already-collapsing signal
   419	joins, the action stack saturates fast. The transformer self-attention, feed-forward, and reduce-sum
   420	stages track the summarizer to within 2 to 11% and introduce no additional collapse; they are passive
   421	relays. The post-encoder projection (vision_post, 1.89x at alpha=1) and the non-temporal hydra trunk
   422	(hydra_trunk, 2.71x at alpha=1) show no cliff, consistent with the two surviving heads of E1. The
   423	localization is partial: the summarizer ends in a mu-over-sigma variational reparameterization, and we
   424	have not separated the mu path from the sigma normalization, so part of the apparent summarizer collapse
   425	could be variance normalization rather than information loss. We state this ambiguity rather than claim
   426	a complete mechanistic account. Figures E5a and E5b show the per-stage and per-submodule ratios.
   427	
   428	### 5.6 E6: Monitor detection and baseline comparison
   429	
   430	We now test whether the signal the outputs hide is recoverable from the recurrent feature, and how a
   431	second-order monitor of that feature compares to the location-based scores one would default to.
   432	
   433	The threshold-free comparison is at alpha=1.0 (full CARLA shift), on the in-distribution set of subaru
   434	and ram concatenated (n=638 stored frames) against the CARLA OOD frames (n=319 stored frames), with
   435	stratified bootstrap 95% confidence intervals; after the rolling-window warm-up, the metrics are
   436	computed on the 609 and 290 valid scores that remain. The rolling-spread monitor reaches AUROC
   437	0.996 [0.992, 1.000], AUPR 0.995 [0.990, 1.000], and FPR at 95% TPR of 0.000. KNN-50 reaches AUROC
   438	1.000 [1.000, 1.000]. We do not claim to beat KNN-50: on single-corpus separation the two tie. Relative
   439	Mahalanobis reaches AUROC 0.934 [0.914, 0.952]. Mahalanobis (0.159 [0.130, 0.190]) and PCA-Mahalanobis
   440	(0.152 [0.124, 0.179]) both score below chance. Table E6 collects these with their confidence intervals.
   441	
   442	Mahalanobis scores below chance for a mechanical reason worth stating, not just reporting. Under the
   443	collapse the recurrent state freezes to a near-constant vector that lands near the center of the
   444	in-distribution Gaussian (E2), so the OOD frames receive lower Mahalanobis distance than the real
   445	frames: a distance-from-mean score cannot detect collapse-to-the-mean, and inverts. PCA-Mahalanobis
   446	inherits the same inversion.
   447	
   448	The distinguishing axis is not single-corpus separation but cross-corpus calibration, and here the
   449	location-based scores fail to transfer. Under leave-one-corpus-out across subaru and ram, all three
   450	applicable location-based baselines (Mahalanobis, relative Mahalanobis, KNN-50) hit 100% LOCO FPR: a
   451	threshold calibrated on one corpus flags the entirety of the other. The PCA-Mahalanobis variant improves
   452	to 11.91% LOCO mean FPR, a partial recovery but still an order of magnitude above the 1% operating point.
   453	The rolling-spread monitor holds 1.03% LOCO mean FPR (max 2.07%), where we emphasize that this is an N=2
   454	two-fold estimate, not a production false-positive rate. The geometric reason the location-based scores
   455	fail is that the subaru and ram corpora occupy disjoint regions of the 512-D feature space whose
   456	inter-corpus separation dwarfs the within-corpus radius (visible as the two real clusters in Figure E2),
   457	so any absolute-position score calibrated on one corpus flags the whole of the other. The monitor reads
   458	the second-order trace, which is location-invariant, so it both separates and calibrates across the
   459	corpus shift.
   460	

