   460	
   461	The monitor also fires before the outputs cliff. Sweeping alpha, the monitor's fired fraction crosses
   462	50% at alpha=0.550, where the E4 Subaru output cliff is at about alpha=0.784, an early-warning gap of
   463	about 0.23 blend-units. (As Section 5.4 notes, this headroom is Subaru-specific; on the RAM gradient the
   464	firing point falls inside the transition band.) Figures E6a and E6b show the fire rate and the AUROC
   465	trajectory across alpha, and Figure E6c gives the ROC and PR curves at alpha=1.0.
   466	
   467	The honest positioning against the nearest neighbors follows from these numbers. Against the
   468	location-based family (Keser-style density, Lee Mahalanobis, Ren RMD, Sun KNN), the result is not that
   469	the monitor separates better, since KNN-50 ties it, but that the location-based scores fail to transfer
   470	across corpora (100% LOCO FPR) while the second-order monitor calibrates (about 1%, N=2). Against
   471	EigenTrack (arXiv:2509.15735), the nearest mechanism-twin, the deltas are substrate (a shipped driving
   472	model, not an LLM or VLM), statistic (a single location-invariant spread trace with a calibrated
   473	threshold, not a full eigenspectrum fed to a trained classifier), and evaluation axis (cross-corpus LOCO
   474	FPR on real-driving frames, not language OOD benchmarks). We frame the result as transfer and
   475	calibration, not as outperforming baselines.
   476	
   477	### 5.7 E7: Corruption sweep (two-way bound)
   478	
   479	The final experiment bounds the contribution in both directions: it bounds the failure mode (is the
   480	silent collapse a general model-robustness failure, or sim-specific?) and it bounds the monitor (is the
   481	monitor a general OOD detector, or collapse-specific?). We apply the 15 ImageNet-C corruptions at 5
   482	severities each to the real Subaru frames (in raw RGB, before YUV conversion), re-run supercombo with the
   483	correct recurrent-state handling on each corrupted sequence, and evaluate the monitor and the baselines,
   484	with a validation gate that first reproduces the published CARLA collapse (7 of 10 heads) before any
   485	corruption result is read.
   486	
   487	**The collapse is sim-specific.** Running the same E1 output-collapse metric on every corruption-severity
   488	cell, no ImageNet-C corruption reproduces the output collapse: 0 of 75 cells reach the collapse criterion
   489	(5 or more of 10 heads), and the maximum on any single cell is 1 of 10 heads, against 7 of 10 under CARLA.
   490	The silent collapse is a property of full-sim rendering, not of photometric or blur corruptions of real
   491	frames. One consequence is immediate: there are zero false negatives, because there is no output collapse
   492	anywhere in the sweep for the monitor to miss, so its quiet response on fog, brightness, blur, and the
   493	rest is correct.
   494	
   495	**The monitor is collapse-specific.** On most corruptions the monitor sits near chance. Its mean
   496	per-corruption AUROC is 0.55 on fog, 0.55 on snow, 0.52 on jpeg compression, 0.60 on brightness, and 0.58
   497	on zoom blur, rising only to about 0.68 to 0.71 on the defocus, motion, and glass blur families, all well
   498	below the operating regime it holds on CARLA. The monitor fires on only four of the 75 cells at the
   499	calibrated threshold: frost severity 3 (AUROC 0.958), frost severity 5 (AUROC 1.000), gaussian-noise
   500	severity 4 (AUROC 0.861), and impulse-noise severity 5 (AUROC 0.906). Crucially, all four of those cells
   501	have zero output collapse, so on this corpus the monitor's firings track a recurrent-feature-spread shift,
   502	not the collapse mode. The monitor is therefore correctly quiet on the real-frame corruptions the model
   503	tolerates and is not a universal corruption detector.
   504	
   505	**Baselines under corruption.** The location-based baselines fire on many corruptions at their calibrated
   506	thresholds (Mahalanobis and relative Mahalanobis fire at high rates across fog, frost, several noise
   507	families, snow, contrast, and zoom), but recall from Section 5.6 that both carry 100% LOCO FPR: they also
   508	flag held-out real driving, so their high corruption fire rates are not calibrated detection. KNN-50 fires
   509	only on the heaviest noise and frost. None of the baselines is calibrated to the 1% operating point the
   510	monitor holds.
   511	
   512	**Synthesis.** The corruption sweep narrows the contribution precisely. The silent collapse, the dangerous
   513	mode, is sim-specific; the corruption sweep shows it is not a general model-robustness failure, and the
   514	cell-for-cell overlay shows the monitor is not a general corruption detector. The contribution is a
   515	targeted monitor for the specific, dangerous, sim-induced silent-collapse mode at about a 1% real-driving
   516	FPR (N=2), where output-side and location-based feature scores do not detect it. We do not claim the
   517	monitor generalizes beyond CARLA as a collapse detector, because no non-CARLA output collapse was induced
   518	for it to have caught. Figures E7a, E7b, and E7c give the AUROC heatmap, the severity sweep, and the
   519	cell-for-cell collapse-versus-detection overlay.
   520	
   521	---
   522	
   523	## 6. Limitations
   524	
   525	We state the boundaries that define where the evidence does and does not extend, so that the bounded
   526	finding is not mistaken for a general result.
   527	
   528	**N=1 model.** Every result is on supercombo v0.9.7 alone. No other openpilot version, and no Tesla,
   529	Mobileye, Waymo, or research imitation-learning stack, was tested. The silent-collapse phenomenon and the
   530	monitor are demonstrated on this one model, and we claim no generalization to any other. Whether silent
   531	collapse is a property of this architecture, this training recipe, or end-to-end driving models broadly is
   532	the N>1 study this paper does not attempt.
   533	
   534	**N=2 corpora; LOCO is a two-fold estimate.** The cross-corpus calibration rests on two real corpora.
   535	Leave-one-corpus-out at N=2 is a two-fold estimate whose variance is not meaningfully reportable, and the
   536	roughly 1% FPR is therefore a calibration estimate, not a production false-positive rate. A third real
   537	corpus, at minimum, is needed before any single production FPR can be quoted with an uncertainty.
   538	
   539	**Monitor scope: collapse-specific and offline-only.** The corruption overlay (Section 5.7) shows the
   540	monitor is collapse-specific and near chance on most real-frame corruptions, so it is not a universal OOD
   541	detector. It is demonstrated offline on logged, rendered, and corrupted frames only; no on-road, in-stack,
   542	or real-time deployment was run, and no causal link to field incidents was established. The residual gap is
   543	that no non-CARLA output collapse was ever induced, so the monitor was never tested as a collapse detector
   544	on anything other than CARLA; real adverse-weather footage (rain, night, glare) that actually induces a
   545	non-CARLA collapse remains pending, and is the most reviewer-resistant follow-up.
   546	
   547	**Partial localization.** The collapse is pinned to the summarizer's variational bottleneck and the
   548	action-block feedback path by ruling out the encoder and probing eight submodules, but the mu-versus-sigma
   549	ambiguity inside the summarizer's reparameterization is unresolved, so the localization is partial, not a
   550	complete mechanistic account.
   551	
   552	---
   553	
   554	## 7. Conclusion
   555	
   556	A shipped Level-2 driving model, openpilot v0.9.7 supercombo, shown CARLA-rendered input, collapses to a
   557	plausible near-constant and does not raise its own uncertainty: 8 of 10 output heads fall to under 1% of
   558	real activity, the recurrent state freezes to about 1e-5 of its real spread, and 0 of 219
   559	out-of-distribution frames exceeds the model's real-driving uncertainty p95. A simulation "pass" can
   560	therefore be the model collapsed to a safe-looking default rather than the model perceiving, and the
   561	output-side and uncertainty signals a safety case would trust are exactly the ones that stay silent.
   562	
   563	The signal those outputs hide is recoverable from the model's own recurrent feature with a single
   564	second-order statistic, the rolling temporal spread of the 512-D state: one O(d) quantity per forward pass,
   565	with no retraining and no architecture change. Calibrated leave-one-corpus-out to about a 1% real-driving
   566	FPR (N=2, a two-fold estimate), it separates the collapse at AUROC 0.996 and fires about 0.23 blend-units
   567	before the output cliff on the Subaru source, where the location-based feature scores one would default to
   568	(Mahalanobis, relative Mahalanobis, KNN) each hit 100% leave-one-corpus-out FPR and fail to transfer across
   569	the two real corpora.
   570	
   571	Output-side monitoring alone is insufficient for the safety case of this shipped driving model, and a
   572	second-order recurrent-state monitor is a cheap complement. This is a single-model, collapse-specific,
   573	offline-only result: an ImageNet-C sweep shows the silent collapse is sim-specific and the monitor is
   574	collapse-specific, and the present evidence does not support generalization to other models, OOD axes, or
   575	deployment contexts. Those are the next studies, not this one's claims.
   576	
   577	---
   578	
   579	## 8. Reproducibility Note
   580	
   581	The headline analyses rerun from committed result caches on a fresh public clone without a GPU or CARLA.
   582	The committed caches are `report/teardown_collected.npz` (E1, E2, E3), `report/e4_collected.npz` (the E4
   583	Subaru cliff), `report/baselines_collected.npz` (the Mahalanobis, RMD, and KNN-50 baseline scores), and
   584	`report/metrics_collected.npz` (the E6 AUROC/AUPR/FPR bootstrap table); the ablations regenerate from those
   585	caches as well. The bootstrap is pinned (n=1000, seed=42) and the run environment is pinned in
   586	`requirements.txt`. The parity test reruns from the released openpilot v0.9.7 ONNX and comma's logged
   587	reference, which are fetched separately and are not redistributed in the repo.
   588	
   589	Three supporting result caches are not committed to the public repo because of their size, and the
   590	experiments that read them therefore do not rerun from a fresh clone: the E5 submodule cache
   591	(`report/e5_submodule_collected.npz`, about 98 MB), the E7 corruption cache
   592	(`report/e7_collected.npz`, about 110 MB), and the E4-RAM cache
   593	(`report/e4_ram_collected.npz`, about 28 MB). For each, a regeneration path is documented: the corresponding
   594	`--collect` pass regenerates the cache from the released ONNX, the source frames, and a GPU, after which the
   595	analysis reruns as for the committed experiments. The committed result files (`report/e5_submodule_results.md`,
   596	`report/e7_results.md`, `report/e7_overlay_results.md`, `report/e4_ram_results.md`) and the committed figures
   597	record the outputs of prior successful collection passes and are readable as-is. The large E5 layer-collection
   598	file (`report/e5_collected.npz`, about 3.9 GB) is not committed and is additionally corrupt on the author's
   599	machine; it will be replaced by a small committed summary array (per-stage, per-alpha activity ratios), which
   600	is all the layer analysis reads. The cache-distribution decision is therefore settled: the four headline caches
   601	are committed and reproduce from a fresh clone, and the three smaller supporting caches are regenerated via the
   602	documented `--collect` passes rather than shipped in the public repo.
   603	
   604	---
   605	
   606	## Figure and Table Manifest
   607	
   608	- Figure 1 (hero.png): four-panel overview (output collapse E1, uncertainty silence E3, Subaru cliff E4,
   609	  monitor detection E6).
   610	- Figure E1 (e1_head_collapse.png): per-head CARLA-to-real activity ratio, 8 collapsed, 2 alive.
   611	- Figure E2 (e2_feature_ood.png): projected 512-D feature space, real clusters versus the CARLA freeze point.
   612	- Figure E3 (e3_confidence.png): uncertainty distributions, real versus CARLA, against the real p95.
   613	- Figure E4 (e4_interpolation.png + e4_ram_interpolation.png): Subaru cliff and RAM gradient.
   614	- Figure E5a (e5_layer_localization.png): per-stage activity ratio, encoder at or above real.
   615	- Figure E5b (e5_submodule_localization.png): per-submodule cliff-alpha, summarizer and action block.
   616	- Table E6 (detector comparison): AUROC, AUPR, FPR@95TPR, and LOCO FPR for all five detectors at alpha=1.0
   617	  with 95% CIs.
   618	- Figure E6a (e6_detector.png): monitor fire rate versus alpha, firing before the cliff.
   619	- Figure E6b (auroc_vs_alpha.png): AUROC versus alpha for all five detectors.
   620	- Figure E6c (roc_curves.png + pr_curves.png): ROC and PR curves at alpha=1.0 (appendix candidate).
   621	- Figure E7a (e7_auroc_heatmap.png): 15x5 monitor AUROC heatmap across corruption-severity cells.
